<p>A Nigerian Air Force strike in Jilli, near the Yobe-Borno border in northeastern Nigeria, hit a local market while targeting fighters from Islamic State West Africa Province, according to residents, local officials and rights groups. Amnesty International said more than 100 people were killed and 35 were seriously wounded, while some local accounts put total casualties, including injuries, at around 200. Nigerian military statements said the operation struck a terrorist enclave and logistics hub in the Jilli axis, but did not initially acknowledge civilian casualties. The strike happened in a region that has faced a long insurgency linked to Boko Haram and ISWAP, where civilians have repeatedly been caught between militant attacks and military operations. In a sign of possible accountability steps, the Air Force said it activated its Civilian Harm Accident and Investigation Cell to carry out a fact-finding mission into the reports, even as opposition figures and Amnesty called for a transparent investigation and accountability for those responsible.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/my_u1e24DeLG3QDjs7g4DsywW4K4aS6AwmyW2JyWIoEh-lFp_yHeRruw_Py8sZeZ34gv35BuroFGKAmd9N9zVV0OI_pboZyz1b8JiCvXMyrJa8hmWbL0NGF0MQ1OOqrKGr0Q2CqBz5LUzHNcsXhOEWPaVQWPA8TKBeC1T_xqV8Jvv1t9Rj0fLEbYuNSKxWL3UXe0-Mg6TCtLERP3DuDFEtki3Jv-MKH-pqvGxH1r4kTeJaUTfFdGvMFMdaa0ifSM5JsSl3TRH5-_qADbvQ' alt='FILE - Nigerian Air Force officers display ammunition next to a fighter jet during an event in Makurdi, Nigeria, on April 22, 2017.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Border market: Jilli’s market serves communities around the Yobe-Borno border, and local reports portrayed it as a busy civilian trading point rather than an isolated battlefield site.</li><li>Competing descriptions of the target: Military accounts placed the operation near the abandoned village of Jilli and framed it as a strike on insurgent positions, while residents said the bombs fell on an active market.</li><li>Evolving toll: Casualty estimates shifted as bodies were counted in stages and severely wounded survivors were moved to hospitals in Geidam and Maiduguri, where some later died.</li><li>Saturday-night strike: Residents said the bombs fell on Saturday night as military aircraft hunted fighters, during a period of intensified clashes in northeastern Nigeria.</li></ul><blockquote>The total casualties, dead and injured, is around 200. - Lawan Zanna Nur</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Nigerian Air Force: The Air Force described the mission as precision bombings of terrorists&#x27; locations in the Jilli axis, maintaining that the intended target was militant infrastructure rather than a civilian site. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604130073.html'>AllAfrica / Premium Times</a>)</li><li>Nigerian Army: The army cast the strike as an intelligence-driven operation against a militant network in the Jilli area and highlighted the fighters it said were killed, without addressing reports of civilian deaths. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604130092.html'>AllAfrica / Premium Times</a>)</li><li>Amnesty International: Amnesty condemned the strike as a civilian killing and urged a transparent investigation and accountability for those responsible. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604130083.html'>AllAfrica / Premium Times</a>)</li><li>Atiku Abubakar: The former vice-president condemned the reported market strike and called for urgent accountability from military authorities. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604130088.html'>AllAfrica / Leadership</a>)</li><li>Local residents and officials: Residents and local leaders said the bombs hit a crowded market used by civilians, challenging official claims that the target was only a militant enclave. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/12/world/africa/snigerian-airstrike-insurgents-civilians.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-nigeria-air-force-market-strike/'>At least 100 dead in Nigeria after air force ‘misfire’ hits local market, sources say</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/100-dead-nigeria-after-air-force-misfire-market-131972032'>At least 100 dead in Nigeria after air force &#x27;misfire&#x27; on market, sources say</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604130083.html'>Nigeria: Amnesty International Condemns Killings of Civilians in Military Airstrike</a> - allafrica.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/12/dozens-feared-dead-in-air-strike-on-village-in-northeastern-nigeria'>Dozens feared dead in air strike on village in northeastern Nigeria</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgk021z3ejpo'>Civilians feared killed after reports of air strike on Nigerian market</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/12/world/africa/snigerian-airstrike-insurgents-civilians.html'>Scores Killed in Nigerian Military Strikes as Clashes With Militants Intensify</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1991145/nigeria-air-strikes-leave-scores-of-fighters-dead'>Nigeria air strikes leave scores of ‘fighters’ dead</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/12/nigeria-airstrike-market-yobe-state-boko-haram'>Nigerian airstrike targeting jihadists reportedly kills at least 100 civilians</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/worldnews/comments/1sjxvtc/nigeria_many_dead_after_military_bombs_village/'>Nigeria: Many dead after military bombs village market</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/africa/article/3349822/200-feared-dead-after-nigerian-air-strike-hits-market-northeast-yobe-state'>At least 200 feared dead after Nigerian air strike hits busy village market</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/nigeria-air-force-attack-misfire-market-death-toll-updates/article70854878.ece'>At least 100 people killed in ‘mistaken’ air force attack on Nigerian market</a> - thehindu.com</li></ul>